+ Findings:
+
+ 1. Duck envelope inverts when the master tunable is set below the duck volume. bed_duck_multiplier
+    computes duck_ratio := MUSIC_DUCK_VOLUME / MUSIC_VOLUME with no upper clamp. If the user tunes
+    MUSIC_VOLUME to, say, 0.10 (below MUSIC_DUCK_VOLUME 0.12), the ratio is 1.2 and event sounds make the
+    bed louder (0.10 × 1.2 = 0.12) than its normal level (0.10) — the opposite of a duck. The bed_level
+    comment explicitly blesses this state ("even when the master sits below it") while the constant's own
+    doc promises the bed is lowered under events.
+
+ 2. The "absolute duck floor" claim is false under pause. bed_level applies the pause soft-lower after the
+    duck ratio, so paused+ducked = 0.40 × 0.6 × 0.3 = 0.072 — not the documented "absolute" 0.12. The
+    constant comment ("the absolute bed level while ducked"), the bed_level comment ("the duck floor stays
+    MUSIC_DUCK_VOLUME absolute"), and the README ("ducks the bed to MUSIC_DUCK_VOLUME (0.12)") all
+    describe an invariant the code does not implement in the paused state.
+
+ 3. The envelope math leans on raylib's undocumented SetMusicVolume clamp. The bed_level comment relies on
+    "SetMusicVolume clamps at 1.0 in raylib" as the safety mechanism for the multiplier, instead of
+    clamping in the pure function the unit tests can actually drive — so the tuning edge cases (finding 1)
+    are masked by a version-dependent C-library behavior rather than fixed or tested.
+
+ 4. Missing tests for the two documented edge states. The new suite covers unducked, unducked+paused,
+    ducked, and ramp shape — but never ducked+paused (the 0.072 state where the floor claim breaks) and
+    never MUSIC_VOLUME ≤ MUSIC_DUCK_VOLUME (the inverted-duck state both constants' comments discuss).
+
+ 5. audio_destroy unloads the music stream without rl.StopMusicStream first. The raylib teardown idiom is
+    stop-then-unload; unloading a live streamed context (decode context + audio-thread buffer) while it is
+    playing is riskier than the one-shot path, and this diff introduces it without the stop.
+
+ 6. bed_update has no muted early-out. While muted the stream is paused, yet rl.UpdateMusicStream +
+    rl.SetMusicVolume still run every frame (decode work on a paused stream, per-frame volume churn), and
+    bed_envelope_step keeps advancing — a pre-mute duck finishes its recovery in silence, so unmute can
+    land mid-ramp with no envelope state preserved deliberately.
+
+ 7. The duck trigger re-encodes the flush's play-family list. audio_flush's four-way || condition
+    (crisis_plays || arrival_plays || wire_plays || spawn_plays) duplicates knowledge of every play
+    family; adding a fifth family to the flush later silently misses the duck unless this OR-list is
+    updated in lockstep. No shared "any plays staged" helper or per-family hook exists.
+
+ 8. No fade/ramp on bed start, mute, and unmute. The loop seam is crossfaded and the duck has ramps, but
+    boot starts the bed at full volume instantly and M flips pause/resume instantly — asymmetric with the
+    envelope discipline everywhere else in this change, and a start/stop pop risk.
+
+ 9. The build script has no failure guards and no verifiable output contract. A missing vault WAV is a raw
+    wave.open traceback; a non-s16 (24-bit) source would be silently misparsed (np.frombuffer(...,
+    np.int16) with no sampwidth assertion); a missing ffmpeg is a raw FileNotFoundError traceback. And no
+    checksum/hash of the committed OGG is recorded anywhere, so the "reproducible" claim and the binary
+    itself cannot be cross-checked post-hoc by a reviewer or CI.
+
+ 10. The 24 MB intermediate WAV is guarded only by a print statement. The script says "delete after
+     encode; never commit" but nothing in the payload enforces it — no ignore rule appears in the diff and
+     the script doesn't clean up after itself — so a later git add assets/audio/ can sweep the 24 MB file
+     into the repo.
+
+ 11. Spec/code drift on the proc name. The spec's Tasks section names the pure helper
+     bed_level_multiplier; the code ships bed_duck_multiplier (plus bed_level and bed_envelope_step,
+     neither of which appears in the spec's Tasks list or Code Map) — a reviewer diffing spec against code
+     finds a proc that does not exist.
+
+ 12. bed_envelope_step lacks the bed_on gate its sibling has. bed_duck_trigger guards on bed_on, but the
+     step proc does not — test_bed_off_is_silent_noop passes only because a zeroed Audio has zero envelope
+     state, not because the proc enforces it. Any future path that flips bed_on off with nonzero envelope
+     state (e.g., a reload path) would keep the envelope stepping silently.
+
+ 13. The retrigger-while-deep branch is untested. bed_duck_trigger's documented behavior has two halves
+     ("a retrigger while ducked extends the deep phase; a trigger mid-recovery re-deeps"), but
+     test_bed_duck_retrigger only exercises the mid-recovery branch — the deep-phase reset branch (and its
+     boundary, retrigger at exactly bed_duck_t == 0) is unverified.
+
+ 14. The change set as presented ships a binary asset with no accompanying verification. The payload
+     asserts the OGG is 913,706 bytes and 125.6 s, but contains no checksum and no gate that the committed
+     file matches the documented loop numbers — so the README's measured-claim table (0.048 peak
+     discontinuity, 0.76 similarity, crossfade geometry) is unverifiable against the actual committed
+     bytes by anyone without the vault file.
